  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nHot and dry conditions are expected for our area through the rest of \nthis week and into the beginning of next week, and our dominant \nweather influence will be from a large upper level high. This high \nwill be centered over western North Texas on Wednesday and over \nsouth-central New Mexico on Friday. The high is progged to be \ncentered over the Four Corners area during the weekend into Monday. \nPer the forecast 850mb temperatures and low-level thermal ridging \npattern, Wednesday looks to the hottest day in our area. At this \ntime, forecast highs range from the upper 90s/100 in our southern \ncounties, to 101-105 degrees in the Big Country. We are monitoring \nfor the potential need of a Heat Advisory in portions of our area \nWednesday. Forecast actual highs will be near Heat Advisory criteria \nin some of the Big Country, and forecast heat indices could reach \nHeat Advisory criteria in some of our east-central (Heartland) and \nsoutheastern (Northwest Hill Country) counties. \n\nTriple digit highs are expected in most of the Big Country and some \nof our central counties Thursday through the weekend. Northern and \nwestern parts of the Big Country could have highs at or near the low-\nend threshold of Heat Advisory during that time. Will also be \nmonitoring the tropical system currently in the northeast Gulf, \nwhich is forecast by the National Hurricane Center to track west-\nnorthwest and then west, and eventually toward the Texas coast \nFriday. The forecast track and intensity remain highly uncertain.",
